/**
\addtogroup sparse

The `laplace::sparse` module provides a host-only sparse symmetric \f$L D L^\top\f$ factorization and solver in `<laplace/sparse/sparse_ldlt.hpp>`. Like \ref dense "the dense module", the Eigen backend (Guennebaud and others, 2010) is compiled into its own static library, `laplace_sparse` (CMake target `Laplace::sparse`), as standard C++ and never by `nvcc`, so the public header includes no Eigen types. Functions in this module are not marked `LAPLACE_HD` and run on one host thread per call.

## Matrix format and factorization

\ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt "sparse_ldlt" factorizes a shifted symmetric \f$n \times n\f$ matrix \f$A + \lambda I\f$, where \f$A\f$ is supplied by its lower triangle (diagonal included) in compressed sparse columns:
- `col_ptr` holds \f$n + 1\f$ column offsets into `row_idx` and `values`, with `col_ptr[n]` giving the number of stored lower-triangular entries.
- `row_idx` holds the row index of each stored entry with `row >= column`, sorted in ascending order within each column. When a nonzero shift \f$\lambda\f$ is passed, the diagonal entry of each column must be stored first at `col_ptr[c]`.
- `values` holds the numerical values corresponding to `row_idx`.

Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factorize "factorize()" delegates to Eigen's `SimplicialLDLT` with an approximate minimum degree (AMD) fill-reducing ordering. The sparsity pattern (`n`, `col_ptr`, `row_idx`) is analyzed symbolically on the first call and re-analyzed only when the pattern changes; repeated factorizations with the same sparsity pattern reuse the symbolic ordering and factorization and touch only the numerical values.

The outcome is reported as a \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_outcome "sparse_ldlt_outcome":
- `positive_definite`: Eigen's factorization succeeded, all pivots of \f$D\f$ are finite, and every pivot of \f$D\f$ is strictly positive.
- `not_positive_definite`: Eigen's factorization failed or at least one pivot of \f$D\f$ is zero or negative.
- `non_finite`: an entry of `values` or a computed pivot of \f$D\f$ is non-finite.

After factorization, \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::solve "solve()" solves \f$(A + \lambda I) z = b\f$ in place on a host array `b` of length \f$n\f$ (returning `false` if no valid factorization is currently stored), \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::min_pivot "min_pivot()" and \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::max_pivot "max_pivot()" return the smallest and largest pivots of \f$D\f$ (\f$0.0\f$ for \f$n = 0\f$), and \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factor_nonzeros "factor_nonzeros()" returns the number of stored entries of \f$L\f$ strictly below its unit diagonal (\f$0\f$ before the first factorization).

## Selected inverse

For a factorization \f$P (A + \lambda I) P^\top = L D L^\top\f$, where \f$P\f$ is the AMD permutation, \f$L\f$ is unit lower triangular, and \f$D\f$ is diagonal, write \f$Z = (A + \lambda I)^{-1}\f$. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse "compute_selected_inverse()" computes the entries of \f$Z\f$ on the sparsity pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$ only (fill included) by Takahashi's recursion (Takahashi, Fagan and Chen, 1973; Erisman and Tinney, 1975) over columns \f$j = n - 1, \dots, 0\f$ in the permuted ordering:
\f[
Z_{ij} = -\sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} Z_{ik} L_{kj} \quad (i \in \mathcal{J}_j), \qquad
Z_{jj} = \frac{1}{D_j} - \sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} L_{kj} Z_{kj},
\f]
where \f$\mathcal{J}_j\f$ is the set of row indices of the nonzeros in column \f$j\f$ of \f$L\f$ below the diagonal. Every entry \f$Z_{ik}\f$ read by the sums lies in the pattern because the rows in \f$\mathcal{J}_j\f$ are pairwise joined in the pattern of \f$L\f$; in particular, every stored entry of \f$A\f$ and its transpose lies in the pattern. The recursion costs of the order of one numeric factorization and stores one `double` per entry of \f$L\f$ below the diagonal plus \f$n\f$ diagonal entries.

`compute_selected_inverse()` returns `false` when no factorization is stored, and any later `factorize()` call discards the computed selected inverse. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_entry "selected_inverse_entry(i, j)" takes original (unpermuted) indices in either order and throws `std::out_of_range` if `(i, j)` lies outside the pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$ or if `compute_selected_inverse()` has not run since the last factorization. In `tests/sparse/test_selected_inverse.cpp`, every entry of the pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$ (including fill) is compared against `laplace::dense::spd_solve()` on a \f$1 \times 1\f$ matrix, a tridiagonal matrix, an arrowhead matrix, random sparse matrices (with shift \f$0\f$ and \f$0.75\f$), and a matrix from two crossed groupings; the largest error is \f$8.0 \times 10^{-16} \max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$ on gcc and \f$6.0 \times 10^{-16} \max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$ on CUDA (the host code compiled by gcc and by MSVC), within the test bound of \f$10^{-13} \max_{i,j} |Z_{ij}|\f$.

## Derivative of the selected inverse

Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse_derivative "compute_selected_inverse_derivative(dvalues)" computes the directional derivative \f$\dot Z = -Z \dot A Z\f$ on the sparsity pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$. Here \f$\dot A\f$ is a symmetric matrix given by its lower triangle on the pattern of the last factorized matrix, with `dvalues` aligned with `col_ptr` and `row_idx`. First, a left-looking sweep in the permuted ordering gives the factor derivatives \f$\dot L\f$ and \f$\dot D\f$ on the pattern of \f$L\f$ (with \f$L_{jj} = 1\f$ and \f$\dot L_{jj} = 0\f$):
\f[
\dot L_{ij} D_j + L_{ij} \dot D_j = \dot A_{ij} - \sum_{k < j} \left(\dot L_{ik} L_{jk} D_k + L_{ik} \dot L_{jk} D_k + L_{ik} L_{jk} \dot D_k\right), \qquad i \ge j.
\f]
Then the derivative of Takahashi's recursion over columns \f$j = n - 1, \dots, 0\f$ in the permuted ordering yields \f$\dot Z\f$ on the pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$:
\f[
\dot Z_{ij} = -\sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} \left(\dot Z_{ik} L_{kj} + Z_{ik} \dot L_{kj}\right) \quad (i \in \mathcal{J}_j), \qquad
\dot Z_{jj} = -\frac{\dot D_j}{D_j^2} - \sum_{k \in \mathcal{J}_j} \left(\dot L_{kj} Z_{kj} + L_{kj} \dot Z_{kj}\right).
\f]
Both sweeps read only entries on the pattern. Each direction takes time of the order of one numeric factorization plus one selected inverse, measured in `docs/numerics.md` at \f$0.65\f$ to \f$1.1\f$ times their sum. The derivative storage uses three arrays of the size of \f$L\f$.

`compute_selected_inverse_derivative()` requires `compute_selected_inverse()` to have run since the last factorization and returns `false` otherwise; a later `factorize()` call discards the stored derivative and another call replaces it. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_entry "selected_inverse_derivative_entry(i, j)" takes original indices in either order and throws `std::out_of_range` if `(i, j)` lies outside the pattern of \f$L + L^\top\f$ or if no derivative is stored. Calling \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_dot "selected_inverse_derivative_dot(values)" takes a symmetric matrix \f$V\f$ given by its lower triangle on the pattern of the factorized matrix, aligned like `dvalues`. It returns \f$s = \sum_{i,j} \dot Z_{ij} V_{ij} = -\operatorname{tr}(Z \dot A Z V)\f$, counting each entry below the diagonal twice, and throws `std::out_of_range` when no derivative is stored. In `tests/sparse/test_selected_inverse.cpp`, on the same six matrices, a random symmetric \f$\dot A\f$ and a random symmetric \f$V\f$ on the pattern of \f$A\f$ are checked against dense \f$-Z \dot A Z\f$ and \f$-\operatorname{tr}(Z \dot A Z V)\f$. The largest entrywise error is \f$1.5 \times 10^{-15} \max_{i,j} |\dot Z_{ij}|\f$ and the contraction error is within \f$8 \times 10^{-15}\f$, identical on gcc and CUDA. Both satisfy the test bounds of \f$10^{-12} \max_{i,j} |\dot Z_{ij}|\f$ and \f$10^{-11}(1 + |s|)\f$.

## Example

The following host snippet factorizes a \f$3 \times 3\f$ tridiagonal symmetric positive-definite matrix stored in lower compressed sparse column format and solves \f$A z = b\f$ in place:

```cpp
#include <laplace/sparse/sparse_ldlt.hpp>

#include <array>

bool solve_tridiagonal_example() {
  const int n = 3;
  const std::array<int, 4> col_ptr = {0, 2, 4, 5};
  const std::array<int, 5> row_idx = {0, 1, 1, 2, 2};
  const std::array<double, 5> values = {2.0, -1.0, 2.0, -1.0, 2.0};

  laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt ldlt;
  if (ldlt.factorize(n, col_ptr.data(), row_idx.data(), values.data()) !=
      laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_outcome::positive_definite) {
    return false;
  }

  std::array<double, 3> b = {1.0, 0.0, 1.0};
  return ldlt.solve(b.data());  // b now holds {1.0, 1.0, 1.0}
}
```

## Module types

In namespace `laplace::sparse`:
- \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt_outcome "sparse_ldlt_outcome"
- \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt "sparse_ldlt" (including \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::factor_nonzeros "factor_nonzeros()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse "compute_selected_inverse()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_entry "selected_inverse_entry()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::compute_selected_inverse_derivative "compute_selected_inverse_derivative()", \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_entry "selected_inverse_derivative_entry()", and \ref laplace::sparse::sparse_ldlt::selected_inverse_derivative_dot "selected_inverse_derivative_dot()")

## References

- G. Guennebaud, B. Jacob and others (2010), Eigen v3, C++ template library for linear algebra, version 3.4, https://eigen.tuxfamily.org
*/
